  "Number": 21,
  "Question": "It is given that one root of \\(2x^2+rx+s=0,\\) with \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) real numbers, is \\(3+2i\\) \\((i=\\sqrt{-1}).\\) The value of \\(s\\) is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "undetermined",
    "B": "\\(5\\)",
    "C": "\\(6\\)",
    "D": "\\(-13\\)",
    "E": "\\(26\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Because \\(3+2i\\) is a root, substituting it makes the polynomial zero. This gives an equality whose real and imaginary parts must each be zero. Using \\(i^2=-1\\), first calculate\n\\[\n(3+2i)^2=9+12i+4i^2=5+12i.\n\\]\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n0&=2(5+12i)+r(3+2i)+s\\\\\n&=(10+3r+s)+(24+2r)i.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nSince \\(r\\) and \\(s\\) are real, the imaginary part gives \\(24+2r=0\\), so \\(r=-12\\). The real part then gives\n\\[\n10+3(-12)+s=0,\n\\]\nso \\(s=26\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
